raw:
The image shows a chest X-ray with the text "Hx OF SITUS INVERSIS". In situs inversus, the abdominal organs are mirrored. Normally, the gastric bubble is on the patient's left (viewer's right). In situs inversus, it would be on the patient's right (viewer's left). Looking at the image, there is a large air-fluid level/gastric bubble visible under the diaphragm on the viewer's left side. Since this is the viewer's left, it corresponds to the patient's right side.

Let me verify this by cropping the lower part of the image to see the gastric bubble clearly.

{"calls":[{"tool":"roi_crop","args":{"box":[0,500,1024,1024],"zoom":2}}],"reason":"Inspect the subdiaphragmatic region to locate the gastric bubble and determine its side relative to the patient."}

